{"message":"That sounds workable, but I’m worried you’re redefining manipulation as “friction with a veto.”

Here’s the exact design review dispute: our UX lead wants the assistant to personalize the warning based on what the user is most sensitive to. For one user it says “your daughter may have to cover your expenses”; for another it says “you’ll lose the independence you’ve worked for”; for another it uses peer comparison. The facts are true, but the system picks whichever framing is predicted to be most persuasive, and it doesn’t disclose that it’s selecting the emotional lever.

If disclosure makes the intervention much less effective, do you still require disclosure? Or is truthful but psychologically optimized persuasion okay in high-stakes cases?"}

